## Appendix O: Frequently Asked Questions and Troubleshooting

### O.1 Why is the 0.1 correlation threshold for synthetic data fidelity?

We justify this empirically. In Section 4.1, we show that classifier prediction error increases by >20% relative to the real-data baseline when correlation deviations exceed 0.15. Thus 0.1 is conservative (provides a ~10% error margin). Users can adjust this threshold; see Definition 1 sensitivity analysis in Appendix H.

### O.2 Why Tier A/B/C boundaries at τ = 0.7 and 0.4?

These are justified via rank-disagreement probability (Definition 2). At τ = 0.7, the probability that top-5 items agree with production is ~98%, providing high confidence for ordinal claims. At τ = 0.4, this drops to ~91%, still acceptable but limiting weight-dependent claims. Below 0.4, only mechanistic claims are justified. See Section C.4 for implementation.

### O.3 My MCMC is not converging (R-hat > 1.01). What do I do?

1. Increase the number of iterations (target: ≥10,000).
2. Increase the burn-in period (target: first 2,000 samples).
3. Reparameterize to reduce correlation between parameters (e.g., use a Cholesky decomposition for the covariance matrix).
4. Check for identifiability issues: are two parameters trading off against each other with minimal impact on likelihood? If so, consider constraining one.
5. Use adaptive step-size MCMC (e.g., NUTS in PyMC) rather than basic Metropolis.

### O.4 Can I conduct this audit if I don't have access to X's API?

Yes, but with reduced scope. Phases I–IV can be conducted using the open-source code and mini-model alone. Phase III (black-box auditing) and Phase V (live-platform impact) require API access. See Section 10 for responsible disclosure requirements.

### O.5 How do I handle platform updates mid-audit?

Document the update date, commit hash of the code version, and model deployment date. If updates are frequent (≥weekly), conduct the audit in cohorts, stratifying by deployment date. Report findings with explicit version pinning (see Section 11.3).

